  "summary": "Several preprints this week recover molecular, senescence and niche information from H&E and spatial transcriptomics using foundation and causal models.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is computational extraction of molecular and cellular structure from tissue data. Three preprints treat routine H&E as a molecular readout. Loki2, a single-cell pathology foundation model trained on 15 million cells, tests how much molecular and cellular state H&E encodes. GenVinci reconstructs 3D molecular tissue scenes from histology to map senescence across aging mouse organs and human skin. sc-HistoMorphomics links 194 nuclear morphometric features to spatial transcriptomics. In spatial omics, CIRCA uses causal representation learning to recover identifiable cell- and niche-level programs, including plaque-associated ones. spicyR v2 provides a permutation-free co-localisation test that accounts for image differences and estimates required cohort size. Adjacent work addresses inference reliability: GatorAnchor models uncertainty in multi-omics neighborhoods, and SigMatch shows that sample tissue strongly shapes transcriptomic disease-relationship predictions. Peripheral items cover ECM-based stress and aging signatures (ECMExplainer), Granger-causal analysis of actin regulators, and feedback topologies for restoring heterogeneity during self-renewal.",
